 \begin{abstract}
-Published side-channel defenses protect specific observations and execution environments. Reusing these results in LLM-based workflows requires identifying which guarantees survive changes in receivers, tool state and attacker adaptation. We systematize \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core and \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent), retaining 39 scoped result records and auditing 20 mitigation families. A nine-item reporting audit and three deployment checks bind each result to its target, observer, execution boundary, workload and measured cost. Only 3 of 17 applicable families bind task or component utility, and 2 of 11 bind a matched adaptation contrast. Three controlled experiments illustrate evidence boundaries; seven author-written judgments were recorded beforehand. These cases do not measure the audit's discrimination or external validity. Streaming the same 48 saved responses as per-token TLS records shows that per-event padding leaves verbosity fully separable and identification at 58--62\% (chance 2.1\%). A buffered cap's 2.18$\times$ cost therefore does not price a stream. On 115 retail reference programs, randomized cancellation cover and decoy programs leave learned or count attacks at 77--100\% balanced accuracy, and clean-trained attackers read 50\% where refitted ones read 100\%. On an encrypted agent--tool link, request-only tool identification reaches 89.9--91.4\% under task-grouped held-out evaluation. Shape-equalizing sandboxed cover with constant framing gives 50.0--54.4\% balanced accuracy at 26.3$\times$ bytes and 7.7$\times$ calls, but no tested network configuration meets the evaluated-suite criterion for both targets. These tests replay saved outputs and reference programs and do not evaluate an autonomous agent.
+Side-channel defenses protect particular observations and execution environments. Deploying them in LLM and agent workflows requires connecting their measured protection to the receiving interface, tool state and adaptive attacker. We systematize \FinalN{} study families (\FinalCore{} core and \FinalAdjacent{} adjacent), retaining 39 scoped result records and auditing 20 mitigation families. Our nine-item audit and three deployment checks turn heterogeneous results into explicit comparison obligations; only 3 of 17 applicable families bind task or component utility, and 2 of 11 bind a matched adaptation contrast. Controlled tests expose consequential mismatches: per-event padding leaves streaming verbosity fully separable, clean-trained attackers score 50\% where refitted attackers score 100\%, and request sizes identify retail tools with 89.9--91.4\% held-out accuracy. We extend these tests to real GPT-6.1 Sol agents with defense parameters frozen solely from 414 training runs. An interim evaluation covers 246 test runs on 41 customer-disjoint held-out tasks. Online shadow cover with constant framing reduces maximum network balanced accuracy from 96.7\%/93.4\% to 50.0\%/50.0\% for cancellation/return, at 15.3$\times$ bytes and 3.8$\times$ calls in TLS replay. Workflow rules yield 96.7\% measured compliance but leave intent inference above 92\%. The intervals do not certify chance-level protection, and replay preserves task reward by construction. Together, the source-bound audit and executable experiments identify which observation, state and evaluation bindings determine a defense's measured protection and deployment cost.
 \end{abstract}
 
